  "name": "Question 13",
  "problem": "What is the variance of the following data set?\n\n$$\n-9,\\,0,\\,2,\\,-7,\\,-6\n$$",
  "worked_solution": "If we have a data set that consists of the points ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},\u2026,{x}_{n},$ then the variance can be computed as\n\n$$\n{\\sigma }^{2}=\\overset{\u2015}{{x}^{2}}-(\\overset{\u2015}{x})^{2},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\overset{\u2015}{x}$ is the mean of the data set and $\\overset{\u2015}{{x}^{2}}$ is the mean of the squares of the points in the data set.\n\nFirst, let's compute $\\overset{\u2015}{x}.$ There are $n=5$ points in our data set, so the mean is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\u2015}{x} & =\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}\\cdot (-9+0+2-7-6) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}\\cdot (-20) \\\\  & =-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's compute $\\overset{\u2015}{{x}^{2}}.$ Computing the mean of the squares of the points in the data set, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\u2015}{{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}\\cdot [(-9)^{2}+{0}^{2}+{2}^{2}+(-7)^{2}+(-6)^{2}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}\\cdot [81+0+4+49+36] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}\\cdot 170 \\\\  & =34.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the variance is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sigma }^{2} & =\\overset{\u2015}{{x}^{2}}-(\\overset{\u2015}{x})^{2} \\\\  & =34-{(-4)}^{2} \\\\  & =34-16 \\\\  & =18.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
